      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I got a bit carried away with the goblin analogy. Let's get back to the math.\n\nThe interest accrual on your loan is calculated using a formula that takes into account the outstanding principal balance, the interest rate, and the number of days in the month. The formula is:\n\nInterest = Principal x Rate x Time\n\nWhere:\n\n* Interest is the amount of interest accrued in the month\n* Principal is the outstanding principal balance\n* Rate is the annual interest rate (in decimal form, so 5% would be 0.05)\n* Time is the number of days in the month (assuming a 30-day month for simplicity)\n\nLet's plug in some numbers:\n\nAssuming a $30,000 principal balance and a 5% annual interest rate, and a 30-day month:\n\nInterest = $30,000 x 0.05 x 30/365 ≈ $41.67\n\nSo, in the first month, approximately $41.67 would be interest, and $30,000 - $41.67 = $29,958.33 would be principal.\n\nHowever, since the interest is compounded daily, the actual interest accrued in the first month would be slightly more, as the interest is added to",
